window.CREF_EN={"1-3":"Python's print is more concise: no include headers or main needed.","1-4":"Python uses dynamic typing, so no declarations are needed.","1-5":"In Python, division naturally yields a float.","2-1":"Python requires no type declarations.","3-2":"Python uses indentation where C++ uses braces.","4-1":"Python's def is more concise.","4-3":"Python's closure rules are similar.","5-1":"Python's import works the same way.","6-1":"Python lists are more flexible; you never specify a size.","7-1":"Python lists grow dynamically on their own.","8-1":"Python classes likewise use self where C++ uses this.","9-1":"Python's inheritance syntax is similar.","12-1":"NumPy arrays resemble the C++ vector but are far more powerful.","13-1":"C++ map/unordered_map correspond to Python dict and DataFrame.","15-1":"Python's with open() is safer: the file closes itself.","16-1":"Python's try/except works almost the same as C++ try/catch."};
